{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a significant figure in the history of the Crusader states during the twelfth century. He served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. His life was marked by complex political maneuvering and military struggles against Muslim forces. Raymond inherited the county after the death of his father Raymond II who was assassinated by the Hashashin sect. The young count faced immediate challenges to his authority due to his minority status. Regency councils governed the region until he reached adulthood and assumed full power.\n\n*Early Life and Inheritance*\nRaymond was born around 1140 or 1142 to Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His father fell victim to a plot by the Nizaris while visiting a mosque in Tripoli. This event forced the nobility to appoint a regent named Raymond of Roucy. The regent managed affairs until Raymond III came of age. He traveled to France to seek support and education before returning to rule his domain. His early years were defined by the need to secure his borders and stabilize the internal politics of the county.\n\n*Political Alliances and Conflicts*\nThe count became deeply involved in the succession crisis of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He opposed the ambitions of Raymond of Antioch and later supported the claim of Sibylla. His relationship with King Baldwin IV was particularly strained due to disagreements over regency rights. Raymond eventually sided with Guy of Lusignan against the court party led by Raymond of Antioch. This factionalism weakened the Christian defenses significantly before the Battle of Hattin. He served as a military commander but often found himself at odds with other leaders.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Death*\nThe decisive confrontation occurred in 1187 when Saladin invaded the kingdom. Raymond advised against engaging the Muslim army in open battle yet his counsel we